# Copy this file to scripts/.mirror-codenames (which is gitignored) and fill it
# in with the real private codenames. Consumed by scripts/check-mirror-codenames.sh.
#
# Format: one entry per line, PRIVATE_CODENAME|public-alias|mode
#   - the alias is optional (leave it empty to detect-only)
#   - the mode is optional; omit it for the default match
#   - blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored
#   - PRIVATE_CODENAME is matched case-insensitively, bounded on any
#     non-alphanumeric character, so `client__client-be` matches while
#     `clientele` does not
#
# Modes:
#   (empty)   default. Boundary match only, as described above.
#   compound  ALSO matches the token embedded inside a dotted or underscored
#             machine identifier: a bundle id, a package name, a host name, a
#             double-underscore slug. Use this for a distinctive codename.
#             Do NOT use it for a short token that occurs inside ordinary
#             English words: it will flag the whole tree. Check first by
#             running the guard with the mode set and comparing the file count
#             against the default mode; a distinctive codename adds zero files.
#             A hyphen is not a compound separator, deliberately: hyphenated
#             English prose is shape-identical to a hyphenated identifier.
#   anything else falls back to the default, so an older checkout reading a
#   newer sidecar keeps working.
#
# The real file is gitignored on purpose so no private codename ever lands in
# either repo. This example carries only placeholders.
EXAMPLE_CLIENT_CODENAME|public-alias-to-use-instead|compound
ANOTHER_CODENAME_NO_ALIAS|
SHORT_TOKEN_INSIDE_ENGLISH_WORDS|its-alias
